.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH SANDBOX-EXEC 1 "October 2026" "sandboxed-autograder" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sandbox-exec \- run one command in an isolated, resource-limited sandbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sandbox-exec
.B \-\-cgroup\-root
.I DIR
.B \-\-cpu\-time\-ms
.I N
.B \-\-timeout\-s
.I N
.B \-\-memory\-mb
.I N
.B \-\-max\-processes
.I N
.B \-\-max\-output\-bytes
.I N
.B \-\-tmpfs\-size\-mb
.I N
.RB [ \-\-copy
.IR SRC [: DEST ]]...
.RB [ \-\-run\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.RB [ \-\-result
.IR FILE ]
.B \-\-
.I CMD
.RI [ ARG ...]
.br
.B sandbox-exec \-\-sweep \-\-cgroup\-root
.I DIR
.RB [ \-\-run\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sandbox-exec
runs exactly one command inside new user, PID, mount, network, UTS and IPC
namespaces, in its own cgroup v2 sub-cgroup, behind a seccomp-BPF allowlist, and
writes a JSON report describing how it ended.
It never runs as root and is not setuid.
It knows nothing about test cases or scores; the grading harness calls it once
per test case.
.PP
The program sees a minimal root file system: read-only
.IR /usr ,
.I /bin
and
.I /lib
(or their targets), the loader cache from
.IR /etc ,
a small
.IR /dev ,
a fresh
.IR /proc ,
and a size-capped tmpfs at
.IR /work ,
which is its working directory.
It has no network interfaces and no capabilities.
.PP
Standard input of
.B sandbox-exec
is read to end of file (at most 4 MiB) and forwarded to the program.
The report is written to standard output, or to
.BR \-\-result .
.B sandbox-exec
writes its own diagnostics to standard error only.
.SH OPTIONS
All six limits are required; there are no defaults.
.TP
.BI \-\-cgroup\-root " DIR"
Delegated cgroup under which the per-run sub-cgroup is created.
See
.BR REQUIREMENTS .
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-time\-ms " N"
Cumulative CPU time budget of all processes of the run, checked from
.I cpu.stat
with millisecond precision.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout\-s " N"
Wall-clock limit, independent of the CPU budget.
.TP
.BI \-\-memory\-mb " N"
.IR memory.max ;
.I memory.swap.max
is set to 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-processes " N"
.IR pids.max .
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-output\-bytes " N"
Combined limit for standard output and standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-tmpfs\-size\-mb " N"
Size of the tmpfs mounted at
.IR /work .
Both output files live there too.
.TP
.BI \-\-copy " SRC" [: DEST ]
Copy host file
.I SRC
to
.I /work/DEST
before the run.
.I DEST
is relative, may not contain
.I ..
and defaults to the base name of
.IR SRC .
May be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-dir " DIR"
Directory for the per-run work directory (default
.IR /tmp ).
.TP
.BI \-\-result " FILE"
Write the report to
.I FILE
(mode 0600, symbolic links are not followed) instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-sweep
Remove empty
.I sbx-*
cgroups and leftover run directories of dead runs, then exit.
Run it once when the worker starts.
.TP
.I CMD
Absolute path of the program inside the sandbox, followed by its arguments.
.PP
The limits must satisfy
.PP
.RS
2 * max_output_bytes < tmpfs_size_mb * 2^20
.br
tmpfs_size_mb + 16 <= memory_mb
.RE
.PP
so that a disk-write bomb fills the tmpfs before the cgroup runs out of memory
and is not reported as a memory bomb.
.SH ENVIRONMENT OF THE PROGRAM
The environment of the program is exactly
.IR PATH=/usr/bin:/bin ,
.IR HOME=/work ,
.IR PYTHONHASHSEED=0 ,
.I LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
and
.IR TZ=UTC .
Nothing is inherited from the caller, and no descriptor other than 0, 1 and 2 is
passed on.
.PP
For Python use
.IR "python3 \-s \-P \-B" .
Do not use
.BR \-I :
it implies
.BR \-E ,
which makes the interpreter ignore
.I PYTHONHASHSEED
and brings back run-to-run differences in
.B set
ordering.
.SH REPORT
One JSON document (UTF-8; invalid bytes in program output become U+FFFD) with
these members:
.TP
.B schema_version
Currently 1.
.TP
.B termination_reason
One of the values below.
.TP
.BR exit_code ", " signal
Exit status, or the number of the killing signal; the other is
.BR null .
Both come from
.BR wait4 (2),
never from program output.
.TP
.BR wall_ms ", " cpu_ms
Elapsed time and total CPU time of the cgroup.
.TP
.BR memory_kb ", " memory_source
Peak memory.
.B memory_source
is
.B CGROUP_PEAK
(\fImemory.peak\fR),
.B CGROUP_SAMPLED
(highest sampled \fImemory.current\fR) or
.B RUSAGE
(last resort).
.TP
.BR stdout ", " stderr ", " output_truncated
Captured output, at most
.B \-\-max\-output\-bytes
together.
.TP
.B violation
.B {"syscall_nr": N}
for
.BR SECURITY_VIOLATION ,
otherwise
.BR null .
.TP
.B pids_limit_hit
True if a fork failed because of
.IR pids.max .
.TP
.B error
Message for
.BR INTERNAL_ERROR ,
otherwise
.BR null .
.SS Termination reasons
.TP
.B EXITED
The program exited; see
.BR exit_code .
.TP
.B SIGNALED
Killed by a signal not caused by a limit, for example
.BR SIGSEGV .
.TP
.B TIMEOUT_WALL
The wall-clock limit was reached.
.TP
.B TIMEOUT_CPU
The CPU budget was reached.
.TP
.B MEMORY_LIMIT
The cgroup OOM killer ended the run.
.TP
.B OUTPUT_LIMIT
Standard output and standard error together exceeded the limit.
.TP
.B SECURITY_VIOLATION
The program called a syscall outside the allowlist; the call was refused and
the run ended.
.TP
.B INTERNAL_ERROR
The sandbox could not be set up or supervised; the program's result, if any,
must not be graded.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ran and the report was written, whatever became of the program.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, or a limit invariant is violated.
.TP
.B 70
Internal error.
A report with
.B INTERNAL_ERROR
is written when possible.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Linux 5.10 or later with the cgroup v2 unified hierarchy and unprivileged user
namespaces; libseccomp 2.5 or later.
.PP
.I DIR
for
.B \-\-cgroup\-root
must be owned by the service user and have the
.IR memory ,
.I pids
and
.I cpu
controllers enabled in its
.IR cgroup.subtree_control .
It must hold no processes itself, and
.B sandbox-exec
must run below it, because moving the child needs write access to
.I cgroup.procs
of the common ancestor.
With systemd, give the worker unit
.IR Delegate=yes ,
move the worker into a leaf cgroup at start-up, then enable the controllers.
.PP
On Ubuntu 24.04 and later set
.I kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0
or install an AppArmor profile for
.BR sandbox-exec ;
otherwise
.BR mount (2)
fails with EPERM.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a Python program with 5 as input:
.PP
.nf
.RS
printf '5\\n' | sandbox-exec \\
    \-\-cgroup\-root "$CG" \-\-cpu\-time\-ms 2000 \-\-timeout\-s 5 \\
    \-\-memory\-mb 64 \-\-max\-processes 5 \\
    \-\-max\-output\-bytes 1048576 \-\-tmpfs\-size\-mb 32 \\
    \-\-copy solution.py:main.py \\
    \-\- /usr/bin/python3 \-s \-P \-B main.py
.RE
.fi
.SH BUGS
There is no
.B SCMP_ACT_TRACE
fallback: on a kernel without seccomp user notification
.B sandbox-exec
refuses to run.
.PP
The program is PID 1 of its namespace, so a signal it sends to itself with
.BR kill (2)
is ignored by the kernel; such a run ends at the wall-clock limit.
.PP
POSIX semaphores need
.IR /dev/shm ,
which is not mounted, so Python's
.B multiprocessing.Lock
and
.B Pool
do not work.
.PP
The restriction to the standard library is a policy, not enforced: packages
installed under
.I /usr
stay importable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR namespaces (7),
.BR cgroups (7),
.BR user_namespaces (7),
.BR seccomp (2),
.BR seccomp_unotify (2),
.BR clone3 (2),
.BR pivot_root (2),
.BR mount_setattr (2)
